WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:05.000
The price I'm watching hasn't got there. Why is the alarm going off?

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:13.298
The last traded price isn't always the price used to judge liquidation.

00:00:13.298 --> 00:00:16.947
Many perpetuals assess risk using a mark price and margin rules.

00:00:16.947 --> 00:00:19.570
Check which rules your platform actually uses.

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:39.114
When the price moves against the position, losses eat into equity.

00:00:39.114 --> 00:00:42.802
Near the maintenance requirement, the system may cancel orders,

00:00:42.802 --> 00:00:47.310
reduce the position or force-close it. It won't necessarily wait for your OK.

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:10.871
Liquidation usually means this forced-close risk event,

00:01:10.871 --> 00:01:13.043
not your phone's button breaking.

00:01:13.043 --> 00:01:17.980
The real trigger depends on fees, maintenance margin, margin mode and more.

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:20.350
10x doesn't mean exactly a 10% drop.

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:39.160
It went back up later. Can I just wait for it to come back?

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:41.914
A closed position doesn't come back on its own.

00:01:41.914 --> 00:01:47.450
A drop and then a rise can end very differently for a leveraged position and for plain spot you just held.

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:57.430
Not guaranteed.

00:01:57.730 --> 00:02:01.370
It's not just the destination. It's whether you can survive the path.

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:15.212
Price barely moved, and you didn't close, but your balance dropped.

00:02:15.212 --> 00:02:16.750
The final episode is next.

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:19.390
Understand first, then act.
